  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 123 AM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nVery hot conditions will persist through Monday as 700mb\ntemperatures per ensemble means hover between 17 and 19C across\nwestern Kansas. Low level drying has begun across Oklahoma and\nthis drier air will advect northward today. Thus, heat indices\nwill be at or below the temperatures--between 105 and 109.\nTherefore the heat advisory continues through Monday for all of\ncentral and southwest Kansas. \n\nAn upper level trough over the Great Lakes region will push a \nweak frontal boundary into northern or central Kansas by Monday \nevening before becoming stationary by Tuesday. High temperatures\nwill depend on the exact location of the front and how much \ncool outflow occurs with any thunderstorms. Temperatures from \nDodge City southward may stay near or above 100 through \nWednesday if the front doesn't pass. Along I-70, highs should \nstay in the 90s; but higher humidity in this area will still \nmake it feel very hot. By Thursday and Friday the various \nensemble means show the upper level ridge shifting to the west, \nwith a significant upper level trough over the eastern United \nStates. This will result in northerly mid to upper level flow \nacross western Kansas and allow for cooler air to push southward\nand westward across Kansas. Expect highs in the 90s for later \nnext week into the weekend. Isolated t-storms can't be \ncompletely ruled out; but the vast majority of western Kansas \nwill receive below average rainfall. Any organized storm \nclusters that we often see in late July and early August should \nstay west of Kansas given the light northerly mid level steering\nflow. The grand ensemble shows chances for .25\" or greater of\nrain over the next week around 20% along the Oklahoma state \nline and 40% along I-70; but chances for more substantial rains\nare very low (5-20%).",
